// @title   TropeLang Concept Library — Social and Political
// @version 1.3
// @note    Concepts governing how beings organize, govern, and relate collectively.
//          Covers political drama, historical narrative, institutional conflict,
//          and community stories.
//
// Import: import "trl/concepts/social.trl"


// =============================================================================
// POWER AND GOVERNANCE
// =============================================================================

// The capacity to determine outcomes for others; the ability to shape collective reality.
// Power is relational — it requires something or someone upon which it acts.
concept power          [%Timeless] [+Corrupting] [+Abstract]

// Power that is recognized as legitimate by those subject to it.
// Authority differs from raw power in that it is granted, not only seized.
concept authority      [%Timeless] [+Binding] [+Abstract]

// The supreme legitimate power to govern within a defined territory or domain.
// Sovereignty is both claimed and recognized; it can be contested.
concept sovereignty    [%Timeless] [+Binding] [+Abstract]

// The organized system of decisions and rules that govern collective life.
// Governance may be just or unjust, stable or fragile.
concept governance     [%Timeless] [+Abstract]

// A formal system of rules applied equally across a community.
// Law may or may not align with justice; its authority comes from enforcement.
concept law            [%Timeless] [+Binding] [+Abstract]

// The condition in which rules, patterns, and expectations hold and are predictable.
// Order may be just or unjust; its opposite is chaos.
concept order          [%Timeless] [+Abstract]

// The condition in which established patterns break down; unpredictability prevails.
// Chaos may be destructive, liberating, or creative depending on context.
concept chaos          [%Timeless] [+Abstract] [+Paradoxical]

// The fundamental alteration of the governing order; change at the systemic level.
// Revolution may be violent or nonviolent; it replaces one order with another.
concept revolution     [%Timeless] [+Irreversible] [+Abstract]


// =============================================================================
// JUSTICE AND FAIRNESS
// =============================================================================

// The condition in which people receive what they are due; the alignment of treatment with desert.
// Justice is both a process (how decisions are made) and an outcome (what results).
concept justice        [%Timeless] [+Aspirational] [+Paradoxical] [+Abstract]

// The absence of justice; treatment that fails to give people what they are due.
// Injustice may be deliberate, systemic, or structural.
concept injustice      [%Timeless] [#Painful] [+Abstract]

// The restraint of deserved punishment; treating another better than they deserve.
// Mercy is a choice; it is distinct from justice but may coexist with it.
concept mercy          [%Timeless] [+Aspirational] [+Abstract]

// The application of consequences to actions; the matching of cost to cause.
// Punishment may be just, excessive, or insufficient.
concept punishment     [%Timeless] [+Abstract]

// Responsibility for harm caused — the state of being answerable for one's actions.
// Accountability requires both acknowledgment and consequence.
concept accountability [%Timeless] [+Abstract] [+Binding]


// =============================================================================
// COMMUNITY AND BELONGING
// =============================================================================

// A group of beings united by shared place, purpose, identity, or covenant.
// Community provides belonging and may impose obligation.
concept community      [%Timeless] [+Abstract]

// The alignment of effort or interest between parties toward a shared end.
// Solidarity may be chosen or structural; it is stronger under pressure.
concept solidarity     [%Timeless] [+Abstract]

// The condition of being outside a community — removed, marginalized, or unwelcome.
// Exclusion may be enforced by others or self-imposed.
concept exclusion      [%Timeless] [#Painful] [+Abstract]

// The quality of mutual benefit in relationships — each party gains from the other.
// Reciprocity underlies most social bonds; its violation produces debt or grievance.
concept reciprocity    [%Timeless] [+Abstract]

// The mutual obligation between parties arising from social position or covenant.
// Debt is not only financial — it is any unpaid obligation between beings.
concept debt           [%Timeless] [+Binding] [+Abstract]

// The transfer of one's position, rights, or obligations to another.
// Inheritance is not only material — it includes roles, traumas, and duties.
concept inheritance    [%Timeless] [+Binding] [+Abstract]


// =============================================================================
// TRUTH AND COMMUNICATION
// =============================================================================

// The deliberate creation of false beliefs in another's mind.
// Deception may be through words, omission, action, or context.
concept deception      [%Timeless] [+Corrupting] [+Abstract]

// The condition of being known accurately to others; transparency of self.
// Openness is vulnerable — it creates the possibility of both connection and exploitation.
concept openness       [%Timeless] [+Abstract] [+Fragile]

// The maintenance of information from those who have a stake in it.
// Secrecy may protect or harm depending on what is concealed and why.
concept secrecy        [%Timeless] [+Abstract]

// The withholding of something known; silence in the presence of relevant truth.
// Silence may be protective, complicit, or coerced.
concept silence        [%Timeless] [+Abstract] [+Binding]
